Front Panel puts the firewall's own information on the LCD built into the
front of the appliance, and gives the buttons beside it something to do.

It writes no display code of its own. LCDproc already speaks to these
panels and OPNsense already carries it, so this plugin is the part above:
it generates the server's configuration from a settings page, runs it as
a service, and feeds it screens. Which driver, which serial port, how
many columns and rows and whether the keys are polled are all chosen on
that page, from the thirty-six drivers the LCDproc package installs, so
an appliance with a different panel is a matter of choosing rather than
of waiting for code.

A short table of known appliances is shipped. It matches on SMBIOS and
proposes a driver and its settings, and says plainly that a proposal is
all it is until somebody confirms the screen lit up. Nothing is applied
on installation and nothing starts by itself: a wrong driver writing to
the wrong serial port is not a mistake a plugin should make on its own
initiative.

Every speed is adjustable: how long each screen is shown, how fast a long
line scrolls, how often the display is redrawn from scratch, and the bus
delay to raise when characters go missing. The shipped screens are the
hostname and LAN address, the WAN address and its gateway, throughput on
a chosen interface, CPU, memory and uptime, the CPU temperature where a
sensor exists, a fixed message of your own, and - only where Link Health
happens to be installed - the worst port and its verdict.

The keys are polled rather than volunteered, and not every panel answers.
The page shows what the driver last reported, so a user on unknown
hardware can find out in ten seconds instead of guessing, and the screens
rotate on a timer whether the keys work or not.

